  <ym-title|Independent review of variable-stage comparison
  windows><label|independent-review-of-variable-stage-comparison-windows>

  Reviewed <verbatim|17-variable-windows.tex> against the installed Chapter11
  response, covariance and extraction arguments and the prescribed-radius
  demands in Chapter17. This is a review of this extension, not a fresh
  certification of the entire marked expansion.

  The pre-ratio split has identifiable suppliers: the
  difference-through-response row estimate explicitly fixes R before L
  (Chapter11 near <verbatim|finite-covariance>); the leading polynomial
  recurrence fixes its coefficient amplification before selecting L (the
  paragraph choosing kappa,q,D); the marked interpolation/column estimate has
  its ratio floor selected subsequently. Fixed-ratio stationary jets,
  incidence and path constants remain entry-delay costs. The product estimate
  log product(C+2) \<less\>= sup epsilon_stage times total logarithmic window
  length consequently gives H^{o(1)} even when the number of crossed stages
  is unbounded: every level in the window, hence its minimum stage, tends to
  infinity.

  The adjacent covariance identity <verbatim|ch11:conditional-curvature>
  retains both actual neighboring metrics and is explicitly valid across a
  stage boundary. Iteration therefore telescopes with products of unequal
  actual responses; no common fictitious metric is required. The separate
  actual-history finite-covariance estimate and protected-path error bounds
  are the ones used at the endpoints. The top extraction T is defined by the
  original insertion and its same-insertion covariance and is independent of
  middle/output stops, as stated in the finite-arity proof; restricting one
  longest restarted history is consistent with this definition.

  The window length O(log H) is smaller than the restarted-history short
  interval; overshoots from L_i \<less\>= log(beta_hat_i) are o(log H). The
  positive comparison-error powers and strict strip/mesh inequalities leave
  room for H^{o(1)} losses. The all-copy root exponent can be weakened from
  1/(16 n) to 1/(32 n). The phase conclusion uses the previously proved
  compression-algebra moment norm, rather than a new phase-dependent
  estimate.

  Requested two editorial clarifications from the author: write the stage
  ratio as mathscr L_j, with J_i=J(i+1), to distinguish it from the level
  ratio L_i; explicitly state that post-ratio constants enter additive local
  errors or a bounded number of endpoint factors, not a product once per
  step. Entry bounds by H^{epsilon_j} alone would not justify such a product
  over O(log H) steps. The current recurrence references support the intended
  additive use; no additional mathematical failure was identified in this
  check.
</body>

